Good, the files exist. Now let me analyze the diff more carefully. Let me look at the import path more carefully:

The home.scss file uses `@use '../theme/variables' as gt;` which means it's going up one level from `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/home/` to `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/theme/` and importing `variables`. But the actual file is `_variables.scss`. This is correct SCSS usage (the underscore prefix is implicit in `@use`).

Now let me review all the findings:

## Analysis Complete

Based on my review of the git diff, I found the following issues:

---

**Severity**: Medium

**Finding**: Inconsistent selector pattern in SCSS — the `.cta` block uses an attribute selector `[tuiButton]` which will override all Taiga UI button appearances globally for any element with that attribute. This is problematic because:
1. The appearance attribute is being set to "accent" in the HTML, but the CSS completely overrides it with a custom gradient
2. This makes the `appearance="accent"` declaration in the HTML meaningless (the CSS wins regardless of the appearance value)
3. If other buttons are ever added with `tuiButton`, they will unintentionally inherit this orange gradient

**Fix**: Instead of styling all `[tuiButton]` elements generically, target the specific CTA button using the data-testid. Replace lines 60-70 in home.scss with:

```scss
[data-testid='cta-learn'] {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, gt.$accent-orange, gt.$accent-orange-light);
  border: none;
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 1.125rem;
  padding: 0.75rem 2rem;

  &:hover {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, gt.$accent-orange-light, gt.$accent-orange);
  }
}
```

This way, the styling is scoped to only the CTA button, and the appearance attribute in the HTML becomes semantically meaningful.

---

All other aspects of the diff are clean:
- Naming conventions are followed (kebab-case for file names, proper TypeScript/HTML)
- HTML markup is consistent with existing patterns
- Test description was improved (changed "to the learn page" to "to /learn" for specificity)
- Test assertion was made stricter (changed `.toBeTruthy()` to `.toBe('/learn')`)

